# Pronouns Reference Content

Cases in action - the words you'll use most frequently in Greek.

## Purpose

Show pronouns as the practical application of cases. High-frequency forms that demonstrate how cases work in daily speech.

## Key Insight

English packs every job into one word ("me"). Greek picks a different form depending on what the pronoun is doing, which is where case shows up.

---

## Hero

<hero component="ReferenceHero" file="tabs/pronouns.tsx">
<title>The words that carry half your sentences.</title>
</hero>

## The Split (first band)

<insight name="me-split" heading='One English "me", three Greek forms.'>
ME_SPLIT rows (local to `components/pronouns-section.tsx`), each with a role badge in its case colour:

| Form  | Role badge      | Job                                   | Example    |
|-------|-----------------|---------------------------------------|------------|
| με    | Target          | before the verb                       | με βλέπεις (you see me) |
| μου   | Owner           | after the noun, or "to me"            | μου λέει (tells me) |
| εμένα | Target (strong) | after a preposition, or for emphasis  | για εμένα (for me) |

Followed by a note card: "Target and Owner are the daily drivers." Subject pronouns (εγώ, εσύ…) exist but Greek usually drops them.
</insight>

---

## Pronoun Paradigms (band: "All the forms")

Each paradigm is a `ParadigmLookup`: a LookupCard whose `chip` is the learner handle (Target / Owner / Doer) and whose `eyebrow` is the grammar term (Accusative / Genitive / Nominative), wrapping a `PronounParadigmTable` (person rows x singular/plural columns). Example pills (`ExamplePills`) colour only the pronoun, via each example's `marked` field.

### Target (Accusative) - Object Pronouns

<paradigm name="accusative-pronouns" chip="Target" eyebrow="Accusative">
<rule>Goes BEFORE the verb: σε βλέπω = I see you.</rule>

| Person | Singular      | Plural        |
|--------|---------------|---------------|
| 1st    | με (me)       | μας (us)      |
| 2nd    | σε (you)      | σας (you pl.) |
| 3rd m  | τον (him/it)  | τους (them)   |
| 3rd f  | την (her/it)  | τις (them)    |
| 3rd n  | το (it)       | τα (them)     |

<examples>
- με βλέπεις; = do you see me?
- σε αγαπώ = I love you
- τον ξέρω = I know him
- μας περιμένουν = they're waiting for us
- σας ευχαριστώ = thank you (formal)
</examples>
</paradigm>

### Owner (Genitive) - Possessive Pronouns

<paradigm name="genitive-pronouns" chip="Owner" eyebrow="Genitive">
<rule>Goes AFTER the noun: το σπίτι μου = my house.</rule>
<note>Neuter uses the same form as masculine (του)</note>

| Person | Singular     | Plural        |
|--------|--------------|---------------|
| 1st    | μου (my)     | μας (our)     |
| 2nd    | σου (your)   | σας (your pl.)|
| 3rd m  | του (his)    | τους (their)  |
| 3rd f  | της (her)    | τους (their)  |
| 3rd n  | του (its)    | τους (their)  |

<examples>
- το σπίτι μου = my house
- η μητέρα σου = your mother
- ο φίλος του = his friend
- το σπίτι μας = our house
- τα παιδιά τους = their children
</examples>
</paradigm>

Between Owner and Doer sits the shared "Article or pronoun?" callout (see below).

### Doer (Nominative) - Subject Pronouns

<paradigm name="nominative-pronouns" chip="Doer" eyebrow="Nominative">
<rule>Usually dropped: the verb ending already shows who. Use only for emphasis or contrast. No example pills.</rule>

| Person | Singular       | Plural         |
|--------|----------------|----------------|
| 1st    | εγώ (I)        | εμείς (we)     |
| 2nd    | εσύ (you)      | εσείς (you pl.)|
| 3rd m  | αυτός (he)     | αυτοί (they)   |
| 3rd f  | αυτή (she)     | αυτές (they)   |
| 3rd n  | αυτό (it)      | αυτά (they)    |
</paradigm>

### Weak vs Strong (Target forms)

<paradigm name="weak-strong" heading="με vs εμένα — same meaning, different jobs">
<rule>The Target form has a short (weak) version that clips onto verbs and a long (strong) version that stands on its own: after a preposition, or for emphasis. The table pairs OBJECT_PRONOUNS with EMPHATIC_PRONOUNS row by row, both in the Target colour.</rule>

| English           | Weak (before verb) | Strong (after prep / emphasis) |
|-------------------|--------------------|--------------------------------|
| me / us           | με · μας           | εμένα · εμάς                   |
| you / you (pl.)   | σε · σας           | εσένα · εσάς                   |
| him/it / them     | τον · τους         | αυτόν · αυτούς                 |
| her/it / them     | την · τις          | αυτήν · αυτές                  |
| it / them         | το · τα            | αυτό · αυτά                    |

Then a weak example (με βλέπεις) beside a strong one (για εμένα), and "More strong-form phrases" pills:
<examples>
- για μένα = for me
- με σένα = with you
- για αυτόν = for him
- για μας = for us
- για αυτό = that's why / for this reason
</examples>
</paradigm>

