## Double Object Pattern (Collapsible)

<pattern name="double-object" expandable="true" default="collapsed" heading="Two-pronoun word order (advanced)">
<title>"Give me it" word order</title>
<explanation>Greek puts the person before the thing</explanation>

| English          | Greek       | Literal        |
|------------------|-------------|----------------|
| give it to me    | δώσε μου το | give to-me it  |
| tell it to me    | πες μου το  | tell to-me it  |
| bring it to her  | φέρε της το | bring to-her it|

</pattern>

---

## Clitic Placement (band: "Weak pronouns hug the verb.")

<section name="clitic-placement" anchor="#clitic-placement">
Greek puts object pronouns before the verb, except in commands. Four rules (CLITIC_RULES):
1. Default, before the verb: Με βλέπει.
2. Imperative, after the verb: Δες με!
3. After θα or να: Θα με δει. / Θέλω να με δεις.
4. After δεν: Δεν με βλέπει.

Rule of thumb: whatever signals tense or mood (θα, να, δεν) goes outside, the pronoun goes inside. Imperatives are the one exception.
</section>

